  "name": "Plumage Signaling",
  "aka": [],
  "tag": "custom",
  "facts": [
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Plumage Signaling is the primary means of nonverbal communication among the owl-folk of the Tsardom of Moscavia, in which meaning is conveyed through the deliberate raising, settling, fanning, and positioning of feathers on the body, wings, and head tufts."
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Every owl-folk possesses plumage that is unique to them, ranging from tawny golds and pale greys to deep browns and snowy whites, and the coloration of one's feathers is considered a matter of deep personal identity."
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The feathered tufts atop an owl-folk's head, often called 'ears' though they are not true ears, are among the most expressive elements used in Plumage Signaling, serving as visible indicators of mood, intent, and emotional state."
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "A fully raised crest of head feathers signals alertness, surprise, or heightened attention, while a flattened crest indicates submission, calm, or grief."
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Fanning the wings partially while keeping the body still is a gesture of display used to communicate authority, confidence, or a formal declaration of intent, commonly seen when members of the Nocturnal Duma wish to speak without words during deliberations."
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The ruffling of breast and shoulder feathers in a rapid, agitated pattern conveys irritation or disagreement, and is frequently observed during heated debates in the Grand Owl Hall."
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Smoothing all feathers flat against the body is a gesture of acknowledgment or assent, often used in place of verbal agreement during formal proceedings of the Night Court."
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Among the aristocratic families of Velikoye, mastery of Plumage Signaling is considered essential to social competence, and children of the old human-era noble houses are trained in its subtleties from a young age."
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The custom emerged naturally in the years following the Turning of 998, as the transformed population developed new modes of expression suited to their changed bodies, and it has since become deeply embedded in Moscavian social and political life."
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "In the Grand Owl Hall, where all sessions of the Nocturnal Duma are conducted in darkness, Plumage Signaling supplements the members' keen night-vision and acute hearing, providing an additional channel of communication in an environment where spoken words can carry unintended echoes off the vast stone walls."
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Certain combinations of feather movements have been codified into formal protocol within the Night Court, with specific gestures reserved for particular occasions, such as the opening and closing of sessions, the announcement of a vote, and the declaration of a Tsar's tiebreaking decision."
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Orthodox Church of the Northern Light has blessed Plumage Signaling as a divinely sanctioned gift, interpreting the transformation of feathers as evidence of God's continued presence in the lives of the owl-folk, and incorporating feather gestures into certain liturgical rites."
    }
  ],
