  "facts": [
    {
      "category": "history",
      "text": "In the spring of 997, a coalition of human noble houses led by Prince Yury of Novgorod and the boyar Kazarin launched a coordinated revolt against Tsar Dmitri Rurikovich III, protesting what they called the 'unnatural tyranny' of owl-folk rule and demanding the restoration of human governance."
    },
    {
      "category": "history",
      "text": "The uprising, known as the Human Dawn Conspiracy, sought to seize control of Velikigrad by exploiting the fact that the owl-folk had not yet fully adapted the capital's defenses for aerial combat, relying instead on hastily reinforced ground fortifications."
    },
    {
      "category": "history",
      "text": "The conspirators secured the loyalty of several provincial garrisons and the merchant quarter of Velikigrad, but their plans were betrayed by Metropolitan Ilya of the Orthodox Church of the Northern Light, who had secretly pledged his allegiance to the Tsar."
    },
    {
      "category": "history",
      "text": "Tsar Dmitri Rurikovich III personally commanded the Taloned Host's suppression of the revolt, deploying aerial squadrons to cut off the human rebels' retreat along the Khorol River valley and besieging their stronghold in the old Kremlin quarter of Velikigrad."
    },
    {
      "category": "history",
      "text": "Prince Yury of Novgorod was executed publicly on the steps of the Duma Hall in Velikigrad on 12 August 997, and the surviving human noble conspirators were stripped of their titles and lands, which were redistributed among loyalist owl-folk families, an act that profoundly reshaped the aristocratic hierarchy of the Tsardom."
    }
  ]
}

