  "name": "Plumage Signaling",
  "aka": [
    {"name": "Signaling by Plumage", "tag": "proper", "everyday": false},
    {"name": "feather-speech", "tag": "common", "everyday": true}
  ],
  "tag": "custom",
  "facts": [
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Plumage Signaling — known colloquially as feather-speech — is the practice among the owl-folk of the Tsardom of Moscavia by which meaning is communicated through the deliberate raising, settling, ruffling, and smoothing of body feathers, supplemented by the position and motion of the feathered ear-tufts atop the head.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "It is considered the most socially refined and expressive form of communication among the owl-folk, valued for its nuance and the emotional authenticity of its expressions — feathers, unlike words, are thought to betray the true state of the speaker's heart.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "A speaker signals by fluffing out specific patches of plumage — the breast, the shoulders, the ear-tufts, the tail, or the wing-bar — each region carrying its own semantic weight, much as different parts of the human face convey distinct emotions.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Raised breast feathers, for instance, signal openness or vulnerability; puffed wing-bars suggest readiness or alertness; a slow, deliberate smoothing of the ear-tufts indicates calm contemplation or a measured response.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The custom is so deeply embedded in owl-folk social life that even young nestlings instinctively ruffle their feathers when distressed or flatten them when seeking comfort, suggesting that the signaling capacity was present from the moment of the Transformation in 998.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "At the Night Court in Velikoye, plumage signaling plays a formal role in proceedings: members are expected to modulate their feathers during debate, and certain ceremonial gestures — such as the Nochnoy Predsedatel spreading both wings in a slow, controlled fan before pronouncing judgment — carry the force of ritual.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Court has on at least two occasions passed amendments to the Pravda Tsarskaya specifically addressing the legal implications of plumage signaling — most notably in the matter of involuntary signals, which are now treated as inadmissible evidence in criminal proceedings, on the grounds that an owl-folk cannot always control which feathers rise when.",
      "since": 1000,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Among the common gestures taught in the educational institutions of Velikoye, the 'greeting fan' — a brief, upward sweep of the secondary wing feathers accompanied by a slight forward tilt of the ear-tufts — serves as a polite salutation equivalent to a human handshake or bow.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Conversely, the 'warning ruffle,' in which an owl-folk bristles the feathers along the entire length of both arms simultaneously, is understood across the Tsardom as a clear signal of displeasure or disagreement, and is considered impolite to direct at one's social superiors without verbal accompaniment.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Scholars of the Kruzhok Mudykh have theorized that plumage signaling may have existed in rudimentary form among the human population before the Transformation, pointing to the universal human habit of 'puffing up' with anger or 'shrinking' with fear as evidence of a deep ancestral connection between feathered expression and emotional state.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Holy Patriarchate has taken no official position on whether plumage signaling constitutes a form of divine communication, though several Patriarchs have remarked in public homilies that 'the feathers speak as clearly as any tongue, and perhaps more honestly.'",
      "held_by": null
    }
  ],
