  "name": "Wattles",
  "aka": [],
  "tag": "custom",
  "facts": [
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Among the owl-folk of Velikoye, wattles are pendulous ornaments made from braided leather, silk ribbon, or finely plaited cord, worn hanging from the beak or affixed to the breast feathers just below the chin.",
      "since": 998,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "A wattle is crafted to match or complement the wearer's plumage coloration, though some owl-folk choose deliberate contrasts — a tawny owl might wear a dark violet wattle, or a snowy-white citizen might don a crimson one.",
      "since": 998,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Wattles serve as a form of personal identification and social signaling in Velikoye, functioning much like a coat of arms or family crest, though they are individual rather than hereditary.",
      "since": 998,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Each wattle is registered with the *Pravda Tsarskaya* Bureau of Plumage and Accouterment, which maintains a ledger — updated nightly — recording every wattle's color, material, pattern, and the owl-folk to whom it is assigned.",
      "since": 999,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "An owl-folk may own multiple wattles and change them seasonally or for different occasions, but only one may be worn at a time in public spaces; wearing an unregistered wattle or a wattle belonging to another citizen is a minor offense under the *Pravda*.",
      "since": 999,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "At the winter markets, specialized artisans known as *wattlemakers* set up stalls along the eastern quarter's craft row, where they display their wares on wooden racks and take custom orders based on a client's plumage and personal taste.",
      "since": 999,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The craft of wattle-making is considered a respected, if modest, trade in Velikoye, taught through apprenticeship and governed by the Artisans' Guild of the Eastern Quarter, which sets standards for materials, stitching, and durability.",
      "since": 999,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "During the Transformation of 998, when the entire population of Velikoye was changed into owl-folk overnight, many former citizens found themselves without any means of personal identification — no names visible upon their bodies, no clothing that fit their new forms — and the wattle system was established in part to restore order and individual recognition in a city thrown into chaos.",
      "since": 998,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Wattles are also worn by members of the Night Court as markers of rank and office: judges wear deep indigo wattles threaded with silver cord, while petitioners before the court wear simple undyed leather wattles to signify their equal standing as citizens.",
      "since": 999,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Among the clergy of the Holy Patriarchate, wattles are traditionally black and unadorned, reflecting the belief that divine service requires the renunciation of personal vanity, though minor clerical ranks may add a thin gold or copper thread.",
      "since": 999,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Children born after the Transformation — the first generation of owl-folk who never knew human form — receive their first wattle at the age of their initial flight test, typically between six and eight months of age, in a ceremony overseen by the local quarter's *roosting mother* or elder.",
      "since": 999,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The word 'wattle' itself is believed to derive from the Old Slavic *vätŭlŭ*, meaning 'pendant' or 'hanging ornament,' and it entered common speech in Velikoye within months of the Transformation, replacing the earlier human practice of wearing brooches or medallions.",
      "since": 998,
      "held_by": null
    }
  ],
